architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "sheni_02"
  title: Simple types delegate to the standard crates, and reading them normalises
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-28"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    ADR sheni-0001 fixed the primitive group and, with it, a rule: a literal
    is checked against a closed set of accepted forms, never guessed at. That
    rule is easy to hold when the forms are decimal digits and four boolean
    spellings. The simple group is where it gets expensive.

    A simple type is an interpretation laid over a primitive carrier -- a
    date, an email address, a URL. Every one of them is governed by a
    published standard, and every one of those standards is bigger than it
    looks. RFC 5322 addressing, the WHATWG URL algorithm, and RFC 5952's
    canonical IPv6 text form are each a project on their own, and a
    hand-written approximation of any of them is a bug waiting to be found by
    the first address or address-shaped thing that does not fit the
    approximation.

    The crate README already names a crate per standard. Taking those crates
    at their word is the obvious move, but it is not a free one: they were
    written to be permissive where the standards are permissive, and a probe
    of each one turned up spellings this project would not otherwise accept.
    The `time` crate reads `+2026-08-28` as an expanded year. The `uuid`
    crate reads braced, URN, and unhyphenated forms alongside the canonical
    one. RFC 3339 itself sanctions a lowercase `t` separator and a trailing
    space. Delegating validation means inheriting all of that unless
    something is done about it.

    There is also a conflict inside the README to settle. Its outline writes
    a time as `THH:MM:SS`, with a leading designator; its standards table
    writes `14:30:00`, without one. Both cannot be the accepted form.
  decision: |
    Implement fourteen simple types, each an interpretation over the `string`
    carrier, and delegate validation to the crate the README's standards
    table names for that standard. Where the table lists alternatives, pick
    one: `time` rather than `chrono`, because this layer never needs a system
    clock, a local timezone, or a timezone database, and `time` with default
    features off carries none of that machinery.

    Delegation is qualified by two rules.

    First, Sheni pre-checks what a delegate would wave through but
    sheni-0001 has already ruled out. A leading `+` on a date is rejected:
    sheni-0001's leading-plus rule is about any number-shaped literal, and a
    year is one. A UUID must be in the canonical hyphenated form, so the
    braced, URN, and unhyphenated spellings the `uuid` crate also accepts are
    rejected -- and rejected with their own code, distinct from malformed
    input, because they are recognisable and wrong rather than unreadable.

    Second, reading a simple type normalises, and that is a real difference
    from the layer below. A primitive value is the text, so the text is
    preserved exactly. A simple value is the thing the text denotes, so what
    is preserved is the meaning, and the canonical form is what Sheni emits
    back. `2001:0DB8::1` reads as `2001:db8::1` per RFC 5952, `90m` reads as
    `1h 30m`, and `https://example.com` reads as `https://example.com/`
    because the WHATWG algorithm says the path is there whether it was typed
    or not. None of these changes what the value is; requiring the input text
    to survive unchanged would reject `2026-08-28T14:30:00.500Z`, whose
    trailing zero is correct, and that is too high a price for a tidier rule.

    Duration is three types, not one: `duration` for the IEC 61131-3 form the
    README's outline gives (`T#5s500ms`), `duration_iso` for ISO 8601
    (`P1DT2H30M`), and `duration_human` for the humantime convention
    (`1h30m`). A single type sniffing which of the three it had been handed
    would be exactly the guessing sheni-0001 exists to prevent. The IEC form
    is hand-written, as the standards table predicted, since no established
    crate implements it.

    `time` takes `HH:MM:SS`, resolving the README's internal conflict in
    favour of the standards table, which is the half that names the standard.
    `timestamp` is implemented although the outline omits it, because the
    table names it and RFC 3339 is what most schemas actually reach for.

    `id` is not implemented as a type. The outline pairs it with UUID and
    describes it as based on an integer or a string, which makes it a role a
    field plays rather than a shape a value has. The carrier type the schema
    declares is the validation; there is nothing left for an `id` type to
    check. `uuid` is the type.

    `file_name`, `path`, and `language` are hand-written, the table naming no
    crate for them, and all three are text-shape checks only -- nothing in
    this layer touches a filesystem or resolves a name.

    `language` follows the outline's `en` and `en.EN`. This is a divergence
    worth stating plainly rather than leaving in a table. BCP 47 writes a
    language and region as `en-GB`, with a hyphen and a real region subtag;
    the README writes `en.EN`, with a dot and a region that is not a country
    code. The specification given is the one implemented, but a document
    written today with `en.EN` will not be readable by anything that speaks
    BCP 47, and this is the decision here most likely to be superseded.

    Errors take the 201-299 band. Where a failure mirrors one in the
    primitive band, it takes the mirrored low digits and carries its band in
    its name, so `SimpleLeadingPlus` at 206 sits under `LeadingPlus` at 106.
  outcome: |
    Probing the delegates before writing this down was what turned it from a
    one-line decision into a qualified one. Three of the seven crates accept
    input that would have walked straight past the discipline sheni-0001
    established, and none of that is visible from the standards table alone.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - A standard is implemented by the people who implement that standard, and an address that RFC 5322 permits is accepted whether or not anyone here anticipated it.
      - The pre-checks are few and named, so what Sheni adds on top of a delegate is a short list a reader can audit rather than a reimplementation to review.
      - Splitting duration three ways means a schema says which convention it means, and a document written for one convention cannot be silently read as another.
      - Normalising on read gives every simple value one canonical text form, which makes comparison and round-tripping well defined without a per-type equality rule.
    negative:
      - Seven third-party crates are now a compatibility surface. A permissive change in any of them widens what Sheni accepts, and nothing but the pinned tests will notice.
      - Normalising means the text a document was written with is not always the text it reads back as. That is a genuine surprise for anyone expecting the verbatim behaviour of the primitive layer.
      - The IEC 61131-3 parser is ours, so it is the one duration form with no upstream to defer to and the one most likely to disagree with a PLC toolchain.
      - Following the README on `language` ships a form that no other tool will recognise. It is the decision here most likely to be superseded.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Hand-write every validator instead of taking dependencies
      reason: An approximation of RFC 5322 or the WHATWG URL algorithm is a defect surface with no upper bound, and the README already named the crates. The dependency cost is real but it is smaller than the correctness cost.
    -
      name: chrono rather than time for date, time, and timestamp
      reason: Both parse what is needed. `time` with default features off pulls in no local-timezone or system-clock machinery, none of which this layer uses, and its parsing is strict about component widths without extra configuration.
    -
      name: One duration type that detects which of the three forms it was given
      reason: Format sniffing is the guessing sheni-0001 rejected, one level up. `PT1M` and `1m` differing in meaning by a factor of sixty is the kind of thing a sniffer gets wrong quietly.
    -
      name: Require the input text to survive a read unchanged, for every type
      reason: It would reject correct input -- a trailing zero in a fractional second, a URL written without its root path, an uppercase hex group in an IPv6 address. Strictness that rejects conforming documents is not strictness, it is a bug.
    -
      name: Implement `id` as a simple type
      reason: The outline defines it as being carried by an integer or a string, which is a statement about which carrier a schema picks, not a constraint to check. An `id` type would have no validation to perform.
    -
      name: BCP 47 for the language type
      reason: It is the right standard and it is not what the README specifies. Following the specification given, and recording the divergence loudly, keeps the decision visible instead of quietly overriding it.
